  <div class="takeaway-grid">
    <div class="takeaway-card">
      <div class="tc-icon">◈</div>
      <div class="tc-title">Long-Run Return Case Remains Strong</div>
      <div class="tc-body">A 12.38% CAGR over 25+ years with a Sharpe-like ratio of 0.70 compares favorably with most commodity indices. The dominant driver has shifted from jewelry/industrial demand to macro/reserve-diversification flows, which are structurally persistent.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway-card">
      <div class="tc-icon">◈</div>
      <div class="tc-title">Volatility Regime is the Primary Risk Variable</div>
      <div class="tc-body">With avg vol ranging from ~10% (low-regime) to ~35%+ (crisis), position sizing for gold must be regime-conditional. Use rolling 30d vol for dynamic position scaling; don't rely on long-run vol for real-time risk management.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway-card">
      <div class="tc-icon">◈</div>
      <div class="tc-title">Fat Tails Demand Non-Gaussian Risk Models</div>
      <div class="tc-body">Kurtosis of 7.0 means standard VaR will underestimate tail exposure by ~20–25%. Stress tests should assume a daily loss capability of −5% to −8% during crisis regimes. EVT or t-distribution approaches are essential for accurate tail risk quantification.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway-card">
      <div class="tc-icon">◈</div>
      <div class="tc-title">Seasonal Timing Offers Modest Edge</div>
      <div class="tc-body">January and August seasonal strength is statistically significant (t &gt; 2.0), but effect sizes are small (~0.09–0.14% per day). Seasonal signals should be used as tiebreakers in tactical allocation rather than primary entry signals.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway-card">
      <div class="tc-icon">◈</div>
      <div class="tc-title">MA Crossover as Regime Filter</div>
      <div class="tc-body">The 50d/200d MA crossover has cleanly identified all major bull/bear transitions in this dataset. In a current regime where 50d &gt; 200d and both are rising, the trend-following case is active. Risk management should focus on drawdown limits rather than timing exits.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway-card">
